---
layout: article
title: Privacy
description: What Appwrite Analytics collects and stores for each event, how visitor IDs work without cookies, and the controls you have over tracking and data.
---

Appwrite Analytics works without cookies and doesn't store IP addresses. This page lists what it collects, what it keeps, and the controls available, to help describe your analytics accurately to your users.

# No cookies or stored visitor identifiers {% #no-cookies %}

Appwrite doesn't set cookies for analytics, and the `Tracking` class in the Appwrite SDKs doesn't keep visitor identifiers on the device. In the browser, it doesn't write to `localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, or any other storage. In an app, it keeps only a marker with the app's version, to spot installs and updates. See [Track a mobile app](/docs/products/analytics/mobile#automatic-events). Each event is a single request that carries the event's details.

Appwrite identifies visitors from the request instead. See [Visitor IDs](#visitor-ids).

# What Appwrite stores {% #stored %}

For each event, Appwrite stores:

- **The event**: its name, the time it arrived, and any props you send.
- **The page**: the URL's hostname and path. The query string and fragment are removed, after Appwrite reads UTM parameters from the query string.
- **The referrer**: the referrer URL as the client sent it. Most browsers send only the referring site's origin, such as `https://www.google.com/`, when a visitor comes from another site.
- **The location**: country, region, and city, derived from the IP address.
- **The device**: the full user agent, and the browser, operating system, and device type derived from it. Also the screen size category from the viewport width.
- **Engagement**: scroll depth and engagement time, when the client reports them.
- **Identifiers**: a visitor ID and a session ID, which are hashes, and a user ID, when the event has one.
- **Bot details**: whether the client is a bot, and its name and category.

Appwrite uses the IP address to look up the location, to check whether it belongs to a datacenter network, and to compute the visitor ID. It's also part of the per-IP rate limit, which keeps it for one minute. The IP address isn't stored with the event.

# Visitor IDs {% #visitor-ids %}

A visitor ID is a one-way hash of the property ID, the event's hostname, the visitor's details, and a salt: a random value that changes every day at midnight UTC. The visitor's details are the user ID when the event has one, or else the IP address and user agent. A new salt each day gives the same visitor a new ID, so Appwrite can't follow a visitor from one day to the next. Each day's salt is deleted two days later.

Visitor IDs don't appear on the dashboard, in exports, or in API responses. For how IDs shape your numbers, see [Visitors and sessions](/docs/products/analytics/visitors).

# Data you send {% #data-you-send %}

Event names, props, URLs, and user IDs hold whatever your code sends. Keep personal data such as names, email addresses, and phone numbers out of them. In URLs, that includes query parameters: Appwrite drops the query string from the stored path, but it keeps the full URL of a referrer.

Events carry user IDs in two cases only: your server sends one with an API key, or the property's **Identify signed-in users** setting is on and the event comes from a signed-in session. The setting is off by default. Turn it on only if your privacy policy covers linking analytics to user accounts. See [Properties](/docs/products/analytics/properties#user-identification).

# Do Not Track {% #do-not-track %}

The Web SDK's `Tracking` class sends no events for visitors who have Do Not Track turned on in their browser. It checks the Do Not Track setting only, not Global Privacy Control. To count these visitors too, set the `respectDoNotTrack` option to `false`, or the `data-respect-dnt` attribute of the script tag to `"false"`.

It also sends nothing from automated browsers, such as test runs with Playwright, Puppeteer, Selenium, or Cypress. This check can't be turned off. See [Track a website](/docs/products/analytics/web#do-not-track).

Over REST or from your server, honoring the visitor's choices is up to your code.

# Retention and deletion {% #retention %}

Appwrite stores analytics events for 180 days on every plan, then deletes them. Your plan's retention period sets how much of that history the dashboard shows. See [Limits and retention](/docs/products/analytics/limits#retention).

Deleting a property permanently deletes every event and visit stored for it. To stop collecting without deleting, turn off **Tracking** in the property's **Settings** tab. Appwrite then rejects new events and keeps the existing data. See [Properties](/docs/products/analytics/properties).
